WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.802
-My first guests tonight are
co-hosts of "Morning Joe,"

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:06.172
which airs weekday mornings
at 6:00 a.m. on MSNBC.

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:09.575
Here are Joe Scarborough
and Mika Brzezinski!

00:00:09.575 --> 00:00:11.377
-Hey, how you doing?
-Nice to see you.

00:00:11.377 --> 00:00:13.246
There's a lot I want to
talk to you both about.

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:17.550
First of all, thank you for just
surviving this election cycle.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.820
This has been pretty crazy.
-Or a presidency.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.123
-Yeah, no, it's been
a crazy four or five years,

00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:26.092
so I think all of us
are exhausted.

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:29.462
We actually thought
before 2016 --

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:32.198
we told our boss,
Phil Griffin, at MSNBC.

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:35.301
I said, "Listen, after Hillary
wins, this is exhausting."

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:36.469
-Take a break.
-"We're going to take --

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:38.104
we're going to take
about a two-month break."

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:40.172
-[ Laughs ]
-And that never happened.

00:00:40.172 --> 00:00:41.608
-Never.
-No.

00:00:41.608 --> 00:00:44.977
And I also want to mention,
congrats on your anniversary.

00:00:44.977 --> 00:00:46.946
Is it two years married
for you guys?

00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:48.281
-[ Laughs ]
-Well, you know,

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:49.982
it's very interesting, Jimmy,
you say that.

00:00:49.982 --> 00:00:51.884
Actually, I know that.
-Thank you!

00:00:51.884 --> 00:00:53.786
I know that too.
-And you know that.

00:00:53.786 --> 00:00:54.887
-I know that.
-Uh-oh.

00:00:54.887 --> 00:00:56.956
-But right before
we came on the show,

00:00:56.956 --> 00:00:58.357
I turned to Mika...
-No, I know that.

00:00:58.357 --> 00:01:00.326
-...and I said, "Hey, do you
know what today is, honey?"

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:03.195
And she said, "Uh, no, why?"
-No, come on.

00:01:03.195 --> 00:01:06.432
-Oh, my goodness.
-It's our two-year anniversary.

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:08.768
She still doesn't know
what month I was born in.

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:10.303
So that's my birthday.
-Listen.

00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:11.804
-So it doesn't matter.
-When we did the show

00:01:11.804 --> 00:01:14.507
this morning and I said
what the date was to everybody,

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:16.809
something hit me
that it was a special day.

00:01:16.809 --> 00:01:18.110
-You knew it was special.
-No.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:19.846
-Yeah.
-It was something about it.

00:01:19.846 --> 00:01:21.848
-Something.
-Some of us -- Some of us knew

00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:23.416
it was special,
and it really is now.

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:26.619
Now it's doubly special.

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:28.488
Congrats on that, guys.

00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:31.424
I love seeing you guys together.

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:32.691
I love seeing you
separately as well.

00:01:32.691 --> 00:01:35.328
But together, it's unbelievable.
It's magic.

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:38.297
You guys were hosting
a special Saturday edition.

00:01:38.297 --> 00:01:41.033
They kept adding these special
editions of your show,

00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:43.035
of "Morning Joe."
-Yeah, it just kept going.

00:01:43.035 --> 00:01:45.271
-And, man, I honestly --
I applaud you.

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:46.772
Thank you so much.

00:01:46.772 --> 00:01:50.209
The American people thank you
for just being on the air.

00:01:50.209 --> 00:01:52.712
But you -- MSNBC made the call

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:55.047
that Joe Biden
had become President-elect.

00:01:55.047 --> 00:01:58.851
Joe, what was it like for you
to make that announcement?

00:01:58.851 --> 00:02:00.486
-Well, you know,
something happened to me

00:02:00.486 --> 00:02:02.889
that's never happened before
in like -- what? --

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:04.623
17 years in television.

00:02:04.623 --> 00:02:08.794
I had somebody say into my ear
that Joe Biden is going...

00:02:08.794 --> 00:02:10.663
-Alex.
-...to be the 46th President

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:12.932
of the United States.
Our EP Alex said that.

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:15.901
And I was supposed to, you know,
do the Brian Williams --

00:02:15.901 --> 00:02:17.570
"We have just received..."
-He's good at it.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:20.440
-"...word that Joe Biden

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:23.742
will be the next President
of the United States."

00:02:23.742 --> 00:02:29.081
I got, "Joe Biden is,"
and then I had to stop.

00:02:29.081 --> 00:02:31.918
-And I got worried.
-The last thing I wanted to do

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:34.453
was have anybody hear
any emotion in my voice.

00:02:34.453 --> 00:02:36.623
We were, especially since
it was election coverage,

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:38.691
I wanted to play it
straight down the middle.

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:40.759
Everybody knew
that we were supporting

00:02:40.759 --> 00:02:42.694
Joe Biden over President Trump.

00:02:42.694 --> 00:02:43.996
But at the same time,

00:02:43.996 --> 00:02:46.432
the last thing I wanted to do
was get emotional.

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:48.167
-Start crying.
-With a Cronkite moment,

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:49.368
taking off my glasses.

00:02:49.368 --> 00:02:51.137
So I said, "Mika."
-Yeah.

00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:54.173
-And she said --
then she did the whole thing.

00:02:54.173 --> 00:02:56.509
-Well, your work has paid off.

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:58.478
MSNBC and your show
in particular

00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:00.480
have been fantastic
in the ratings.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.249
So congrats on that if that
makes you feel any better.

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:04.417
But I don't even know
if you care.

00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:05.752
At this point,
you're like, "Let's just --"

00:03:05.752 --> 00:03:08.521
-Nope.
-"We just want a weekend off."

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:09.789
-Yes.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:11.857
-It feels like there's
never been a slow news day

00:03:11.857 --> 00:03:13.926
during Trump's presidency.

00:03:13.926 --> 00:03:15.395
-There hasn't been.

00:03:15.395 --> 00:03:20.199
In fact, since the campaign,
and as Joe said, we wanted --

00:03:20.199 --> 00:03:22.001
every time you would
want to take a break,

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:25.171
every time you go take a break,
it follows you.

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:27.973
And now, literally,
in this pandemic,

00:03:27.973 --> 00:03:29.541
you cannot leave your job.

00:03:29.541 --> 00:03:32.178
It is there with you 24/7.

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:33.746
And with this president,

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:37.416
it has been one you-know-what
after another.

00:03:37.416 --> 00:03:38.985
-Yeah, is this going to
change the pace

00:03:38.985 --> 00:03:43.122
of all news shows in general?

00:03:43.122 --> 00:03:46.492
-I hope. I mean, don't you hope?

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:47.526
-Yeah.

00:03:47.526 --> 00:03:48.828
-I'd take boring.

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:50.896
We can't wait to be boring.

00:03:50.896 --> 00:03:52.999
-[ Laughing ] Yeah.
-Yeah, I mean, I do think,

00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:54.400
though, actually one
of the problems

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.801
over the past four years --

00:03:55.801 --> 00:03:57.970
actually probably over
the past couple of decades --

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.306
is that politics
has become a blood sport.

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:01.607
People turn to their channel.

00:04:01.607 --> 00:04:03.009
-You call it a shock opera.

00:04:03.009 --> 00:04:05.377
-Yeah, it is a shock opera,

00:04:05.377 --> 00:04:08.213
and people root for their team,
root for their side.

00:04:08.213 --> 00:04:10.583
-I think we've been
desensitized as well

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:13.319
to shocking news
from the presidency.

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:16.355
And it's going to take
a long time to unwind this,

00:04:16.355 --> 00:04:17.957
no matter what side
of this you're on.

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:20.125
-Yeah, and both sides
watch your show.

00:04:20.125 --> 00:04:21.427
I will say that, too.

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:23.229
I mean, it's good to have
the conversation with them.

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:24.797
-Absolutely.
-Yeah, it is.

00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:28.735
And I think, you know, we used
to go and give an awful lot

00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:31.036
of speeches to civic groups,

00:04:31.036 --> 00:04:34.740
and we just stopped doing it
over the past several years

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.343
in colleges because things
became so intense.

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:39.712
It used to be
that Mika would get up

00:04:39.712 --> 00:04:42.181
and she would take
the more liberal view.

00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:43.816
I'd take
the more conservative view.

00:04:43.816 --> 00:04:44.950
-We switched all around.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:46.652
-We had this really
fun back-and-forth.

00:04:46.652 --> 00:04:50.356
And let's just say the fun
has gone away a little bit

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:52.925
in the past couple years when it
comes to talking about politics.

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:56.496
Let's hope we can get back to a
place where we can talk civilly

00:04:56.496 --> 00:04:58.731
about it again
and at least be operating

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:00.032
from the same set of facts.

00:05:00.032 --> 00:05:02.501
-[ Chuckles ] Well, Jimmy,
when the show started --

00:05:02.501 --> 00:05:04.704
So, I was sort of
the straight man.

00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:07.406
And then I saw myself
on "Saturday Night Live"

00:05:07.406 --> 00:05:10.376
and realized what's happened
over the past four years

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:13.045
is we've switched roles.
-Yeah.

00:05:13.045 --> 00:05:15.614
-And I've become the crazy one.
-You know, the funny thing is

00:05:15.614 --> 00:05:17.616
everybody called us --
-He's driven me crazy!

00:05:17.616 --> 00:05:19.385
-Not me -- Donald Trump.

00:05:19.385 --> 00:05:21.687
-Do you think that
he is going to concede?

00:05:21.687 --> 00:05:23.122
No, he's not going
to concede, right, Mika?

00:05:23.122 --> 00:05:24.190
-Nope.
-No.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.025
-We have a bet on this.

00:05:26.025 --> 00:05:28.193
That's all I'm going to say,
and I'm going to win.

00:05:28.193 --> 00:05:30.029
And it's a lot because I bet --

00:05:30.029 --> 00:05:31.964
when I bet,
I bet for a lot of money.

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:33.465
-Yeah.

00:05:33.465 --> 00:05:36.335
Joe, why do you think that it's
taking so long for Republicans

00:05:36.335 --> 00:05:39.204
to acknowledge Biden's win?

00:05:39.204 --> 00:05:41.640
-Yeah, I wish I knew.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:44.711
You know, I was --
These people were my colleagues.

00:05:44.711 --> 00:05:45.744
I served with them...
-Your friends.

00:05:45.744 --> 00:05:46.813
-...when I was young.

00:05:46.813 --> 00:05:49.449
When I was 30,
I got elected to Congress

00:05:49.449 --> 00:05:52.051
and I served with
a lot of these people

00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:54.219
for eight years in Congress.

00:05:54.219 --> 00:05:56.255
Seven, eight years in Congress.

00:05:56.255 --> 00:06:00.392
And I've known them for
almost a quarter of a century,

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:02.961
and I just don't recognize
some of them.

00:06:02.961 --> 00:06:05.631
I mean, these are people
that we worked together.

00:06:05.631 --> 00:06:08.367
We fought the good fight
together.

00:06:08.367 --> 00:06:10.970
We were used to -- you know,
there were a lot of times

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:14.406
when many of us were trying to
run Newt Gingrich out of town

00:06:14.406 --> 00:06:18.077
and sort of leading a coup
against his speakership.

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:20.579
And we were doing it
because we didn't feel like

00:06:20.579 --> 00:06:24.216
he was following his word,
that he was keeping his word.

00:06:24.216 --> 00:06:28.987
And to see a lot of these people
just sort of blindly follow

00:06:28.987 --> 00:06:31.624
Donald Trump over the past
four years and say nothing

00:06:31.624 --> 00:06:35.561
when President Trump actually
was talking the last two weeks

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:39.165
of the campaign about
throwing Joe Biden in jail,

00:06:39.165 --> 00:06:41.868
having his attorney general
throw Joe Biden in jail.

00:06:41.868 --> 00:06:43.970
That's something
that happens in Russia.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:45.804
It's bizarre,
and I just kept waiting for

00:06:45.804 --> 00:06:48.241
my friends to speak up,
and they just didn't do it.

00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:49.675
-You never got,
like, a little wink,

00:06:49.675 --> 00:06:52.344
like a little Jack Nicholson,
like "Cuckoo's Nest" like --

00:06:52.344 --> 00:06:54.380
-Yeah.
-Nothing.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:56.115
-Nothing.
-Yeah, they all do.

00:06:56.115 --> 00:06:59.551
No, they will all give you
the little wink off camera.

00:06:59.551 --> 00:07:01.320
And they go, "Yeah, we know
he's crazy. Yeah, yeah.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:04.557
But that really does
none of us any good.

00:07:04.557 --> 00:07:06.792
People needed to start
saying it on camera,

00:07:06.792 --> 00:07:08.193
and for whatever reason,

00:07:08.193 --> 00:07:10.563
they just couldn't bring
themselves to do it.

00:07:10.563 --> 00:07:12.364
-There's more I want to
talk to you guys about.

00:07:12.364 --> 00:07:13.932
Can you stick around
for a couple more minutes?

00:07:13.932 --> 00:07:15.535
-Would love to.
-Absolutely.

